Note = {1st International Conference on Urban Air Quality - Monitoring and
   Modelling, UNIV HERTFORDSHIRE, HATFIELD, ENGLAND, JUL 11-12, 1996},
Organization = {Inst Phys, Environm Phys Grp, UK; Royal Meteorol Soc, UK; Natl Soc Clean
   Air \& Environm Protect, UK; Royal Soc Chem, Uk},
Abstract = {This paper presents a model to forecast concentrations of nitrogen
   dioxide NO2 in urban areas. A box model provides a simple and robust
   description of air quality in urban areas, especially where emissions
   data are only available on a fairly coarse grid. BOXURB is a box model
   with some novel features. It can use synoptic observations of the
   meteorology, or numerical forecasts of wind and cloud. Traffic emissions
   are adjusted by time and day of week, and space heating emissions by
   degree hours. The urban heat flux combines an urban heat store
   adjustment, a space heating term, and the rural heat flux. Pollutants
   accumulate with the onset of light winds. NO2 is forecast by subdividing
   the NOx into its constituents, NO (nitric oxide) and NO2. The
   subdivision is empirical for most of the year, but in ozone episodes the
   photostationary state is invoked. The forecasts are sent by the Met
   Office to NETCEN AEA Technology for use in the Department of
   Environment's Air Quality Bulletin System.},
