   RIGHTS-OF-WAY-MANAGEMENT},
Year = {2002},
Pages = {303-307},
Note = {7th International Symposium on Environmental Concerns in Rights-of-Way
   Management, CALGARY, CANADA, SEP 09-13, 2000},
Organization = {TransCanada; TERA Environ Consultants Ltd; Elect Power Res Inst},
Abstract = {Rights-of-way provide greenway linkages between fragments of natural
   areas. Within an urban context, rights-of-way provide connectivity
   between parks and other protected areas, creating larger breeding
   populations, better gene flow, larger food webs and greater
   opportunities for plants and animals to help each other reproduce.
   Managing rights-of-way to increase biodiversity produces a more
   effective natural network. The Green Links Project, started in 1995,
   focuses on strengthening ecological connectivity within Greater
   Vancouver. This urban area is on the Fraser River Estuary and delta, a
   major stopover point along the Pacific Flyway for migratory birds. The
   Fraser River itself is home to the world's largest salmon run.
   Biological conservation here is of international importance. BC Hydro
   and BC Gas have worked in partnership on the Green Links Project to take
   a regional approach to biological conservation through plantings of
   native vegetation and putting up bird and bat boxes with student and
   community participation in utility corridors, backyards, and balconies.
   Issues and problems that had to be addressed included ownership of
   lands, trees under power lines, and city maintenance crews cutting new
   plantings.},
