   and Villages of the Future, Beijing, PEOPLES R CHINA, NOV 06-09, 2009},
Organization = {Beijing Univ Civil Engn \& Architecture; Water Environm Res Fdn; Natl
   Onsite Wastewater Recycling Assoc},
Abstract = {The China-Sweden Erdos Eco-town Project was a first bold attempt to
   install dry urine-diverting sanitation systems in new multi-story
   buildings at a relatively large-scale level of implementation. The
   project has been in some ways a ``living laboratory{''} for the ca 3000
   inhabitants housed in the 832 apartments in 42 buildings. The design of
   the system was part of a vision for a sustainable urban future with
   improved efficiency in water consumption and reuse of nutrient
   resources. However, to achieve sustainability the various system
   components including technical, institutional and social aspects, need
   further improvements. This paper critically analyses the sustainability
   of the project and offers lessons for future implementation of more
   sustainable sanitation systems.},
