Volume = {25},
Number = {1},
Pages = {65-75},
Month = {FEB},
Note = {7th Motor Control and Human Skill Conference, Fremantle, AUSTRALIA, FEB
   03-07, 2005},
Abstract = {The present study examined the impact of fine and gross motor ability on
   self-perceptions of male and female children and adolescents.
   Participants were compared across age group, sex, and level of motor
   ability. When intercorrelations between self-perceptions were taken into
   account, the level of movement ability was found to impact upon
   perceived athletic competence and scholastic competence. When movement
   was considered in terms of fine and gross motor ability, it was found
   that those with higher perceived scholastic competence were in the
   younger group and had better fine motor skills. Furthermore, those with
   greater perceived athletic competence were also in the younger group,
   were predominantly male and had better gross motor skills. The types of
   self-perceptions that influenced self-worth were dependent on the level
   of motor ability of the participants and varied according to their sex.
   The implications of these findings are discussed with reference to the
   necessity to assess specific types of motor deficit when tailoring
   intervention strategies for children with motor disorders, particularly
   within the academic setting. (c) 2005 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.},
