   1995},
Organization = {Sacramento Municipal Util Dist},
Abstract = {Efforts to increase environmentally sound behaviors and practices have
   in the past often focussed on consciousness-raising and attitude change.
   Research indicates that Such efforts are less effective than
   interventions designed to make environmentally sound behaviors easier to
   engage in, or to make personal advantages resulting from such behaviors
   more clear to individuals. Four nonprofit tree planting organizations
   were studied as examples of successful environmental interventions. From
   these studies, as well as a review of the literature, several principles
   underlying successful behavioral interventions ale identified.
   Implications of these principles for future environmental programs are
   discussed. (C) 1997 Elsevier Science Ltd.},
