Title = {Human perception of local air movement and the acceptable air velocity
   range for local air movement in the tropics},
Booktitle = {INDOOR AIR 2005: PROCEEDINGS OF THE 10TH INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON
   INDOOR AIR QUALITY AND CLIMATE, VOLS 1-5},
Year = {2005},
Pages = {452-457},
Note = {10th International Conference on Indoor Air Quality and Climate (Indoor
   Air 2005), Beijing, PEOPLES R CHINA, SEP 04-09, 2005},
Organization = {Tsinghua Univ; Minist Educ China; Natl Nat Sci Fdn China; Beijing
   Municipal Commiss Sci \& Technol; Shanghai Municipal Commiss Sci \&
   Technol; US EPA; Amer Ind Hyg Assoc; Amer Soc Heating, Refrigerating \&
   Air Conditioning Engineers; Int Commiss Occupat Hlth, SC Indoor Air Qual
   \& Hlth; Int Council Res \& Innovat Bldg \& Construct; European Commiss
   Asia Pro Eco Programme Promot Sustainable Solut Environm Europe \& Asia;
   Korean Soc Indoor Environm; Soc Indoor Environm},
Abstract = {The perception of locally applied airflow from personalized ventilation
   air terminal devices by tropically acclimatized people was studied.
   During the experiments, 24 subjects (male and female) performed normal
   office work in a room equipped with six workstations. They were exposed
   to local airflow from the front and towards the face at six air
   velocities (0.15, 0.3. 0.45, 0.6. 0.75 and 0.9 m/s), at ambient
   temperatures of 26 and 23.5 degrees C and local air temperature of 26,
   23.5 and 21 degrees C. Each combination was maintained for 15 minutes,
   during which the subjects responded to computer-administered
   questionnaires on their thermal and. draft sensations using
   visual-analogue scales.
   The results showed that the subjects preferred air movement within a
   certain range, i.e.. a higher percentage was dissatisfied at both low
   and high velocity values. The perception of air movement of the
   Tropically acclimatized subjects is similar to other human perception,
   e.g., thermal sensation- both cold and heat are perceived as less
   acceptable. The study also identified acceptable air velocity ranges
   from 0.3 up to 0.9 m/s under the experimental conditions. These velocity
   ranges are relevant for design of personalized ventilation in practice.},
